## Key Analysis & Cross-Checks

**Underwriting deterioration is the standout signal.** The **combined ratio of 110%** for Q1FY26 compares unfavorably to 101% in the prior-year comparable quarter (Q1FY25) — a clear underwriting quality red flag despite premium growth. This deterioration is driven almost entirely by claims experience: the **claims ratio rose to 77%** from 67% in Q1FY25, as Net Incurred Claims jumped to ₹1,21,568 lakh (+26.4% YoY) versus ₹96,160 lakh, outpacing premium growth.

**PAT swung to a loss.** Care Health posted a **standalone net loss of -₹14.58 crore** in Q1FY26, versus a profit of +₹20.14 crore in Q1FY25 — a meaningful YoY swing. This flows from an Operating Loss that widened to -₹68.45 crore (vs -₹21.98 crore in Q1FY25), consistent with the claims-ratio spike. Third-party commentary corroborates this pattern, noting H1FY26 cumulative PAT of only ~₹17 crore with "claims growing faster" than premium — implying the weak Q1 was partially offset by a stronger Q2.

**Capital adequacy remains intact.** The **solvency ratio of 1.66x** stays comfortably above the IRDAI-mandated minimum of 1.50x, indicating the insurer retains an adequate capital buffer despite the quarterly operating loss.

**Balance sheet strength.** **Net worth of ₹2,366.64 crore** is derived from the Balance Sheet's Total Sources of Funds (Share Capital + Reserves & Surplus + Fair Value Change Account), which is mathematically equivalent to shareholders' equity here since Borrowings = Nil per FORM NL-11.

**Data limitations.** Retail-specific GWP and market share could not be populated because the statutory FORM NL-4 schedule bifurcates premium only by product line (Health / Personal Accident / Travel), not by Retail vs. Group segment within Health — and no verifiable Q1FY26 investor presentation was accessible to supplement this breakout. Similarly, IFRS-basis PAT and claims ratio are not part of the statutory IGAAP filing and are therefore left blank rather than estimated.</ans>
